ref 17592186045417] [17592186554518 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554518 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554518 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186554043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554518 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d228661a-466c-50ca-9003-6059a883594a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554535 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554535 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554535 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554535 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554535 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186554253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554535 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554535 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554535 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186554171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554535 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f95e685a-8719-5a28-8f48-34b22e58d837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554561 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554561 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554561 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${A}^{T}=A$\n\nII. ${A}^{2}=A$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3561f664-6b98-59a1-983b-c026ed37b09d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554561 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${A}^{T}=A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}20 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{9} & \\frac{10}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10}{9} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since not both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554573 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554573 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554573 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554573 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{12}+{p}_{33}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "555dd099-33d6-5089-b5ce-f831f111dabe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554573 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}\\{a\\}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{12}+{p}_{33}=1+2=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554655 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186554692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554655 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d2cdc91-a374-5d20-8c30-4a54336a8b0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554655 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186272117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554668 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186554655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554668 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40a5ccf0-6aa1-5ea7-b903-ef49b53ecb1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554668 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186554573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554685 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186554668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554685 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "76099d7c-eb32-526b-b5e4-28c871e954f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554685 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186554561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554692 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "822d82b4-c893-527e-9f45-d39dad5654fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554692 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186272122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186554685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a8ac4121-8588-5593-84d3-dac5566df2ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186554719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186134547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921865564