b/ref 1069 "q-40131" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557213 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557213 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557213 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "196a5704-c0f5-5a90-b92b-f7fa820f51b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557213 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557246 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186557171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557246 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557246 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557246 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${A}^{T}=A$\n\nII. ${A}^{2}=A$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557246 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4aff059c-8353-530a-ad6d-89d8d7db0136" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557246 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since ${A}^{T}\\ne A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since not both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
